column8:
The HDIMS will facilitate the establishment of a marine spatial data infrastructure (MSDI) as an integral component of the Philippine National Spatial Data Infrastructure (PNSDI). This ensures a timely and reliable provision of marine geospatial information to the government, private institutions, and the public through a web portal. Marine geospatial information is crucial for making informed decisions and programs and can aid in formulating policies for climate actions, maximizing the use of coastal and marine environments, protecting natural resources, allocating resources, and ensuring national security. Informed decisions and programs in the marine sector will result in the growth of the country's Blue Economy and the attainment of sustainable development goals.

The components of HDIMS are as follows:
-System Maintenance;
-Policies;
-Partnerships, Communication, and Engagement;
-Capacity-Building and Education; and
-Data.

The implementation of HDIMS aligns with PDP Subchapter Outcomes 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, and 1.4. The success of the HDIMS will depend on the participation of various agencies and organizations in creating an integrated marine geodatabase for government and public use. This encourages a whole-of-government and whole-of-nation approach through the use of geospatial information acquired from different contributors and will aid decision and policy makers in managing the marine resources.

This activity covers the inventory of all structures, both vertical and horizontal, permanent and temporary, within forest lands, including but not limited to structures within and outside tenurial instruments. Inventory shall be collected using geospatial technologies through in situ validation using offline data collection tools, UAV photogrammetry, and remote sensing. 

The inventory of structures within forestlands shall be conducted to determine and account for the number of existing structures in forestland, the projection on how built-up areas progress in terms of expansion in the area per year, and the list of factors that may have contributed to the construction of built-up areas inside the forest.

Classification of Structures.
Structures are classified into six (6) categories:
a. Structures within untenured Forestland and without reforestation projects, and
not an initial component of NIPAS, CADT, and Proclaimed areas;
b. Structures within Forestland with forestry tenure agreements;
c. Structures within Forestland and Reforestation Projects;
d. Structures within Forestland and within initial components of NIPAS;
e. Structures within Forestland and within CADT; and
f. Structures within Forestland and within proclaimed areas for special uses.
